            <ArticleTitle>Severe erosion of lumbar vertebral body because of a chronic ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>394-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.spinee.2006.12.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Erosion of vertebral bodies because of abdominal aortic aneurysm is an extremely rare condition. This vertebral destruction is usually seen after aortic graft surgery; nevertheless, it is not expected in primary aortic aneurysms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this article was to present a patient who suffers from back and hip pain because of a chronic ruptured primary aortic aneurysm.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 51-year-old patient had complaints of back pain. Physical examination revealed a pulsatile mass in the periumblical region. By using conventional radiographies, vertebral erosion was detected at the anterior part of L3-L4-L5 vertebrae. A chronic ruptured thrombosed aortic aneurysm was identified by magnetic resonance imaging.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After resection of the aneurysm, it was possible that the lack of anterior column support could result in future instability. Therefore, an L4-L5 anterior partial corpectomy and reconstruction of the anterior defect with titanium mesh cage with posterior instrumentation and fusion were performed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It was concluded that back pain caused by chronic aortic aneurysms is a rare condition and may be one of the possible etiologies in differential diagnosis of low back pain and/or sciatica in some patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
